270: Every boundary primitive has been included. In particular the
271: linear $L$ term comes from the actual $F_{{\rm ext},n}^2$ term and
272: is fixed as an integer by the relative morphism; no modulo-28
273: comparison is used to choose it.
274: 
275: The domain of this local calculation is the explicitly constructed
276: primitive nodal quotient piece and its stated closed seam. Its use
277: for a named global family retains that family's identification of
278: this piece, physical line, cap and parameter orientation. The
279: ordinary and finite-cap source data have not been replaced by
280: an abstract winding matrix in this proof.
281: 
282: ## BW.4. The actual OS49 positive reference is the fan T1
283: 
284: Now retain the actual source and every datum of OR65.1:
285: \[
286:  y^2=x^3-3t^3(t-1)x+2t^4(t-1)(t-\tfrac12),\qquad
287:  G=(t^2,t^2),\quad M=\mathcal O(G-O),\quad Q=3G.
288:                                                         \tag{BW.14}
289: \]
290: The suspension is over $-Q$, with its actual target-point
291: linearization divisor $C_0$ on the $I_2$ source fibre. The
292: $IV^*$ cap has order $m=3k>0$, norm sign $\nu=\pm1$ and
293: fixed character $d\in\mathbb Z$. Its exact ordinary-to-cap
294: arrow is
295: \[
296:  \Phi_r(z,s_0)=z+\frac{\log s_0}{2\pi i}\Pi_r(s_0)
297:                  \{\nu(e_\gamma-e_u)+d\delta\}.
298:                                                         \tag{BW.15}
299: \]
300: The root action is $A_r=r^\nu A_0$. The ordinary closing is
301: the endpoint-to-initial map $q^{-\nu}$; its forward is $q^\nu$.
302: On the type-II source neighbourhood retain the original line
303: complement and contracting suspension. None of these arrows
304: is replaced by a whole-fibre linearization.
305: 
306: The original source and suspension logarithmic periods are
307: \[
308:  u=(2s,s)^t+U(q),\quad
309:  \gamma=(-3s,-s+mv)^t+C(q),\quad
310:  s=\frac{\log q}{2\pi i},\quad v=\frac{\log r}{2\pi i}.
311: \]
312: The actual integral deck-generator change is
313: \[
314:  H=-u-\gamma=(s,-mv)^t-U-C,\qquad
315:  K=3u+2\gamma=(0,s+2mv)^t+D,\quad D=3U+2C.
316:                                                         \tag{BW.16}
317: \]
318: Its determinant is one; these are actual deck transformations,
319: including their bounded source and line units. Thus in the
320: original physical characters the periods are exactly those of
321: BW.1, with
322: \[
323:                    (L,J)=(-m,2m).                    \tag{BW.17}
324: \]
325: The bounded forward is $E_r=(g^{-\nu},\eta^{-\nu})$;
326: the complete seam of BW.5a has $e=\nu,u=1$. Its actual cap
327: square is (BW.15). Hence both signs use the same positive
328: parameter orientation in (BW.17).
329: 
330: Write $T_n=T(e_1+ne_2,e_2)$ in this fixed physical lattice.
331: The infinite negative-diagonal fan is $T_0$. The two triangles
332: of $T_1$ are
333: \[
334:  \{0,e_1+e_2,e_2\},\qquad
335:  \{e_1+e_2,e_2,e_1+2e_2\}.
336: \]
337: The first is a positive-diagonal square triangle; the second
338: is its other triangle translated by $e_2$. Translation by
339: $e_2$ is the full actual $K$ deck, with the unit factors in
340: (BW.16). These equalities hold for every integral translate,
341: so the two infinite fans and all their localized cone rings
342: are literally the same. The dense-character identity descends
343: through the unchanged period action and finite-cap arrows.
344: 
345: The fixed-star charts on $T_n$ use
346: $Z_n=Z,V_n=Z^{-n}V$ and
347: \[
348:  (Z,n_0,n_1)=(Z,q/(ZV_n),V_n),\qquad
349:  (Z',n'_0,n'_1)=(Z^{-1},Zn_0,Zn_1).
350:                                                         \tag{BW.18}
351: \]
352: For $n=1$ they become exactly
353: \[
354:  (Z,A,B)=(Z,q/V,V/Z),\qquad
355:  (Z',A',B')=(Z^{-1},qZ/V,V),
356:                                                         \tag{BW.19}
357: \]
358: the OC.11 positive-fan normal charts. In particular the
359: actual global quaternion is precisely
360: \[
361:  q_H=(1+jZ)(A+j\overline B)
362:      =(Z^{-1}+j)(A'+j\overline {B'}).
363:                                                         \tag{BW.20}
364: \]
365: The physical circle is right multiplication by $e^{-i\phi}$.
366: The decreasing contracting-$K$ height, the constant signed
367: normal reference change for $\nu=-1$, and the full unit
368: normal arrows are OC.12--19. Thus this identification retains
369: the actual Cox cap and its framing, not just the common fan.
370: 
371: The independent positive page is the actual line-complement
372: subfan with heights $\lceil j/2\rceil$ and component degrees
373: $(-1,+1)$. Its $m$ norm shells, cap root and regular bands
374: are OR65.2. The full normal quotient clutch in OC.17--20 has
375: zero normal Pontryagin integral; OC.21 retains
376: $M_{\rm rel}^2=-2k$ and $p_{\rm rel}=-24k$. K68's exact
377: physical/dual line arrow converts its pair to
378: \[
379:                       (k_{M,1},\ell_{M,1})=(2m,8m).
380:                                                         \tag{BW.21}
381: \]
382: The constant $d$-character is carried on every root sheet;
383: its $m$-fold product is one. The relative page line changes
384: only by its already retained base class, whose square and
385: pairing with $M$ vanish. The point-angle determinant-one
386: map in OC.7--10 proves the same signed pair for each $\nu$.
387: 
388: Apply the actual relative calculation (BW.13) in these charts
389: and subtract its value at $n=1$. With (BW.17) and (BW.21)
390: this gives the complete physical pair and index
391: \[
392:  \boxed{\quad k_{M,n}=2m-6m(n-1)^2,\qquad
393:  \ell_{M,n}=8m,\qquad A_{M,n}=-m(n-1)^2.\quad}
394:                                                         \tag{BW.22}
395: \]
396: The dual-$U$ pair is the negative of this pair. In particular
397: the negative fan's integer is now computed by the actual line
398: and tangent morphisms. Its value was not selected from K65's
399: residue. The native fixed-star free pair is
400: \[
401:                (\mu_n,\rho_n)=(2m,-6m(n-1)^2).       \tag{BW.23}
402: \]
403: 
404: ## BW.5. The actual shorter endpoint and the full vertical seed
405: 
406: Retain the exact endpoint of OR65.6. The rational source-line
407: lift $U$ satisfies $\rho=B_0^{-1}/U^3$; its orders at the
408: cusp and finite cap are respectively $1$ and $-1$ modulo
409: three. The rational root $g_0^3=q^{-\nu}\rho^{-2\nu}$ gives
410: the endpoint-to-initial map
411: \[
412:  C_r=r^{2\nu k}g_0U^{-2\nu}:X_{\zeta_3r}\longrightarrow X_r.
413:                                                         \tag{BW.24}
414: \]
415: On the cap the full Cartier map is the invariant unit times
416: $L^{-2\nu}$, with $DLD^{-1}=\zeta_3L$ and $L^3=B_0^{-1}$.
417: Consequently $\Phi_rC_r=C_{\rm cap}\Phi_{\zeta_3r}$ and
418: $C_{\rm cap}A_{\zeta_3r}C_{\rm cap}^{-1}=A_r$. This retains
419: the original $d$-character and both values of $\nu$.
420: 
421: After the actual source deck reindexing, the full bounded
422: closing logarithm and extending origin are
423: \[
424:  c(v)=\frac{\nu}{3}(D+2mv\delta)-\frac{2\nu k}{3}\delta,
425:  \qquad \sigma(v)=\nu vD+\nu m v(v-1)\delta,
426: \]
427: \[
428:  \sigma(v+1/3)-\sigma(v)=c(v),\qquad
429:  \sum_{j=0}^2c(v+j/3)=\nu D+2\nu mv\delta.           \tag{BW.25}
430: \]
431: The forward endpoint is the inverse of this closing. On
432: $T_n$ apply the literal character map $(z,\ell)\mapsto
433: (z,\ell-nz)$ to every vector in (BW.25). This is an
434: integral monomial map and preserves their equalities, all
435: bounded units and the zero-height endpoint. The sheet product
436: $P_j=\exp(2\pi i\sum_{h<j}c(v+h/3))$ is the actual product
437: of the first $j$ closing maps. Its values at $j=0,1,2,3$ are
438: those of OR65.31, with this same character map applied.
439: 
440: For completeness use the fixed native C0 coordinate order
441: $(b_1,a,c_1)$ from K73. A cut multiplier $(A,D_n)$, with
442: $D_n=DA^{-n}$, acts by the factors
443: \[
444:  (A,(AD_n)^{-1},D_n),\qquad
445:  (A^{-1},D_n^{-1},AD_n)
446:                                                         \tag{BW.26}
447: \]
448: on its two normal charts. Its literal Cox lift is
449: \[
450:  (a_0,a_1,c_0,c_1)\longmapsto
451:  (a_0,Aa_1,(AD_n)^{-1}c_0,D_n c_1).                  \tag{BW.27}
452: \]
453: It preserves $q$ and the physical circle. The all-sheet
454: identity in (BW.25) holds on each of these four character
455: factors. Thus the covered $r$ native product is the literal
456: triple pullback of the shorter $w=r^3$ native product,
457: including every collar and the cap square (BW.24).
458: 
459: We also compare that product to the canonical seam used in
460: BW.4. On a linear interior cut the origin product has
461: logarithm $\sigma(v+\theta)-\sigma(v)$, whereas the
462: canonical cut has logarithm
463: $\nu\theta D+2\nu m\theta(v+\theta)\delta$.
464: Their difference is the explicit physical-circle factor
465: \[
466:                  -\nu m(\theta^2+\theta)\delta.
467:                                                         \tag{BW.28}
468: \]
469: Its exponential has value one at both endpoints. It is
470: therefore an actual based global physical-circle action,
471: including its possible winding. It acts identically on the
472: orbit normal three-plane and extends on the same line and
473: Hopf cap. Flatten both cuts on the protected collars using
474: their common actual endpoint relation. The remaining unit
475: ratios have zero endpoint logarithm and the explicit
476: exponential interpolation carries their full jets. This
477: gives the actual relative cap and line comparison between
478: the two covered products; it does not discard a closed
479: circle loop. Hence their completed physical-circle lines
480: and stable tangent bundles are isomorphic with their
481: specified orientation and outer frame.
482: 
483: The degree-three mapping-torus covering now gives literal
484: equalities of characteristic integrals, not division in
485: $\Theta_7$. Writing $j=m/3=k$ on the shorter loop gives
486: \[
487:  (k_{M,n}^{w},\ell_{M,n}^{w})
488:        =(2k-6k(n-1)^2,8k),\qquad
489:  A_{M,n}^{w}=-k(n-1)^2.                              \tag{BW.29}
490: \]
491: The sphere classes are the residues of (BW.22) and (BW.29)
492: by the actual physical Hopf transfer K68.
493: 
494: Here is also the full seed for further exact fan comparisons.
495: Write $j=m$ for the $r$ loop and $j=k$ for the actual shorter
496: $w$ loop. Use the literal doubled ordinary product, carrying
497: the native fixed-star product just specified. Its native
498: double pair is $(4j,-12j(n-1)^2)$. The K70/NV actual C0
499: product change is pure left $-2J=-4j$, so its ordinary
500: double pair is $(0,-12j(n-1)^2)$. Consequently
501: \[
502:  V^{(2)}=\begin{pmatrix}
503:  12j(n-1)^2&8j(1-n)&8j(n-1)\\
504:  8j(1-n)&4j&0\\8j(n-1)&0&4j
505:  \end{pmatrix},\qquad
506:  \delta^{(2)}=\begin{pmatrix}
507:  -12j(n-1)^2\\8j(n-1)\\8j(1-n)
508:  \end{pmatrix}.                                    \tag{BW.30}
509: \]
510: The off-diagonal and remaining diagonal entries come from
511: the full actual trace, not the index. Indeed the doubled
512: period winding in these characters is
513: $B=\left(\begin{smallmatrix}0&0\\2j(2n-1)&4j\end{smallmatrix}\right)$,
514: $t_0=2j$, $w=(0,1,-1)$, $d=(-2,-1,-1)$. The literal
515: normal and derivative prisms of OR65.16--17 give
516: $I=(8j(n-1),4j,-4j)$ and $W=(8j(1-n),0,0)$, which
517: determine every remaining entry of (BW.30).
518: 
519: This result computes every vertical-edge fan with the exact
520: OS49 source, component line, cap and endpoint just retained.
521: It does not substitute these numbers into a previously
522: withdrawn universal scalar-fan expression.
